{
  "raw": {
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3667579/45425?iso=no": {
      "title": "Eksamensassistent (Pakkeansvarlig)",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Eksamensadministrasjonen - Eksamensassistent (Pakkeansvarlig) Eksamensassistent (Pakkeansvarlig) Send application Generelt om pakkingsprosessen Det er to større eksamensgjennomføringer per år ved Handelshøyskolen BI. Disse inntreffer en gang på høstsemesteret, med start i november til og med desember, og en gang på vårsemesteret med start i mai til og med juni. Eksamenen blir gjennomført i lokaler på Campus BI i Nydalen og noen ganger i forskjellige eksterne lokaler rundt omkring i Oslo. Det er stort sett eksamensgjennomføring hver dag i disse periodene med varierende antall eksamener. Ved noen av de ”store” dagene er det nærmere to tusen oppgaver som skal bestilles og pakkes til de forskjellige stedene.  Det krever stor grad av oversikt, nøyaktighet samt detaljkontroll da det er svært viktig at riktig materiale blir distribuert til riktig sted. Hva skal du jobbe med? Arbeidsoppgavene vil i hovedsak bestå av følgende: Bestille eksamensoppgaver fra vår trykkeritjeneste Pakke eksamensoppgaver knyttet til skoleeksamener som avholdes ved campus Oslo Følge opp kursansvarlige i henhold til deres oppsatte eksamener Registrere sykefravær Annet forefallende arbeid Hvem er vi på jakt etter? Vi søker etter pålitelige, ansvarsfulle, serviceinnstilte og engasjerte personer. Det er viktig for oss er at du er nøyaktig og strukturert, har en positiv holdning og er fleksibel, og at du ikke er redd for å bidra der det trengs. Hvordan søke? Registrer deg som bruker i BIs rekrutteringssystem Easycruit og last opp CV og legg ved en kortfattet søknad. Vi ønsker å bli kjent med deg, og i søknaden vil vi gjerne ha svar på følgende punkter: Hvem er du? Hvorfor ønsker du å jobbe hos oss? Hva synes du er spennende med denne jobben? I tillegg inngår det i stillingen og ha kontakt med institutt koordinatorer i forbindelse med purring av eksamensoppgaver og eventuelle korreksjoner. I tillegg må man følge opp med trykkeri for eventuelle henvendelser i forbindelse med bestilling av eksamensoppgaver. Vi ser frem til å motta din søknad. Hvis du har spørsmål angående stillingen, er du velkommen til å kontakte Cristina Parmac på e-post: cristina.s.parmac@bi.no Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 25/10/2026 Expected start date: 02/11/2026 Location: Campus Oslo Contacts: Cristina Parmac Mobile: +47 93497771 Company homepage: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Eksamensadministrasjonen BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3669133/249001?iso=no": {
      "title": "Advisor International Office",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Business Relevance - Advisor International Office Handelshøyskolen BI søker Advisor International Office! Advisor International Office Send application Advisor International Office – temporary position from December 2026-mid April  2028 The International Relations office (IRO) is looking for a structured, proactive and service-oriented professional who enjoys working in an international environment coordinating multiple activities and stakeholders. We are looking for an overall responsible for the BI International Summer School (www.bi.edu/summer). The IRO belongs under the Business Relevance unit where the main focus is on  strengthening  BI’s position as an internationally recognised business school through work in alumni engagement, career development, and international relations. The department is responsible for building lasting relationships, facilitating networks, and enabling students to develop the skills and experience needed for a relevant and sustainable career. The Business Relevance unit connects students, alumni, and business, contributing to BI’s ambition to shape people and businesses for an international and sustainable future. Work tasks Programme planning and delivery Marketing, recruitment and communication Applications, admissions and student administration International partnerships and student mobility Social programme and events Accommodation and housing coordination Fees and financial administration Student experience and support Evaluation, reporting and continuous development Desired skills and qualifications Relevant higher education degree, preferably within higher ed administration, business, communications, or project management Experience with program or project management Experience in working with international students, exchange programmes, or summer schools is an advantage Strong organizational and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple processes, deadlines and stakeholders simultaneously. Excellent communication skills and the ability to communicate clearly and effectively with students, academic staff, administrative colleagues, international partners and external suppliers. Strong service orientation and a genuine interest in creating a positive student experience. Ability to work independently and take ownership of projects from planning through implementation and evaluation. Digital & Data Literacy: Proficient with administrative systems and skilled at using data and student feedback to drive improvements. Service Mindset & Adaptability: Student-focused, service-oriented, and adaptable in a fast-paced environment. Languages: Fluent written and verbal English; Norwegian proficiency is an advantage. Personal qualities We believe you will thrive in this role if you are: Structured and organized , while remaining flexible when plans change. Proactive and solution-oriented , with the ability to identify issues and take action. Collaborative and relationship-oriented , with confidence in working with people from different backgrounds and professional areas. Service-minded and student-focused , with a genuine interest in supporting students and creating meaningful international learning experiences. Independent and accountable , comfortable taking ownership of projects and ensuring that things get done. Curious and improvement-oriented , with an interest in developing new ideas, processes and program offerings. We place great importance on finding the candidate who we believe will be the best fit for our department and who possess the right combination of qualifications and personal qualities. The position reports to Head of the Office, Eline Jensrud, who can also be contacted for further information (tlf 46410224). Application deadline: 20 October 2026 Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Application deadline: 20/10/2026 Expected start date: Etter avtale Location: Oslo Contacts: Eline Jensrud Mobile: +47 46410224 Company homepage: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Business Relevance BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3666269/200453?iso=no": {
      "title": "PhD scholarship in Regulatory Delearning in EU Technology Governance",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad  - PhD scholarship in Regulatory Delearning in EU Technology Governance PhD scholarship in Regulatory Delearning in EU Technology Governance Send application PhD scholarship in Regulatory Delearning in EU Technology Governance BI Norwegian Business School’s Department of Law and Governance and Department of Communication and Culture invite applications for a four-year doctoral scholarship focusing on regulatory delearning and the governance of transformative technologies. The expected start date is 1 January 2027, or upon agreement. Position details Location: BI Norwegian Business School, Oslo, Norway Position: One PhD candidate position, full time, fixed-term contract of 48 months Doctoral programme: PhD specialisation in Communication at BI Norwegian Business School Application deadline: 15 November 2026 at 23:59 CET Expected start: 1 January 2027, or upon agreement About REGULAIRE The scholarship is part of REGULAIRE (Regulatory Learning for the Governance of Transformative Technologies), a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network funded by the European Union under Horizon Europe. Coordinated by Professor Urs Gasser at the Technical University of Munich, with Professor Sebastian Schwemer as principal investigator at BI, the network brings together 15 doctoral candidates at ten European universities. REGULAIRE studies how regulation can learn to govern transformative technologies such as AI, quantum computing and neurotechnology. Its projects examine how regulators recognise emerging problems, test interventions, develop institutional capacities and share lessons across organisations and jurisdictions. You will join an international cohort with supervision across institutions, joint training and collaboration with regulators, standardisation bodies and international organisations. The doctoral project Regulation accumulates over time, but institutions’ capacity to revise or retire rules that no longer serve their purpose does not always keep pace. This doctoral project examines “regulatory delearning”: how institutions identify, revise or retire outdated obligations in response to technological change. Focusing on the EU and EEA, you will investigate how different forms of regulation, from legislation to standards and soft law, influence delearning. You will examine the conditions and procedures for reviewing, amending and repealing rules, including review and sunset clauses, drawing on the EU’s Better Regulation framework and recent simplification initiatives. A central question is how institutions can adapt rules while preserving safeguards, accountability and legal certainty. Possible approaches include legal analysis, comparative case studies, evidence from regulatory sandboxes and comparisons across technology sectors. The successful candidate will develop an independent PhD project within this theme and contribute to REGULAIRE’s research agenda. The project offers a focused route into law and regulation, governance research and the study of adaptive regulation. Research environment and supervision You will be affiliated with BI’s Department of Law and Governance and Department of Communication and Culture. Research in Law and Governance spans national and European regulation of markets and technology, digital and corporate governance, and the relationship between law, economics and public policy. Communication and Culture contributes expertise in communication technologies, online communities, digital transformation and the social legitimacy of technological change. You will also join BI’s Nordic Centre for Internet and Society (NCIS), an interdisciplinary research centre studying how emerging internet technologies affect working life and society. Your main supervisor will be Professor Sebastian Schwemer (BI), with Professor Christian Fieseler as co-supervisor at BI and Stefan Larsson at Lund University as the international academic co-supervisor. A sectoral mentor will support the secondment. You will meet your main supervisor at least monthly and the full supervisory team at least four times a year. Your progress and training will be recorded in a personal Career Development Plan. Training and secondment Network training includes three residential summer schools, covering regulatory learning and foresight at TUM, methods and analytics at BI, and academic writing and impact at the University of St. Gallen. You will also take part in methods workshops, monthly paper development seminars and annual network reviews. This training complements the coursework and other requirements of BI’s doctoral programme. The project includes a planned three-month intersectoral secondment, providing opportunities to study how technology-governance bodies identify, update and retire outdated regulatory mandates. The host, timing and detailed arrangements will be confirmed with the supervisory team. Your work Refine the research questions and design with the supervisory team. Collect, manage and analyse research material using methods appropriate to the project. Complete a doctoral thesis and related research publications, and communicate findings to academic and practitioner audiences. Participate in local and network training, cohort activities, reviews and practitioner engagement, including the summer schools and planned secondment. Contribute to teaching or other career-promoting duties as part of the four-year appointment. Your profile Applicants must hold, or complete by the start date, a relevant two-year master’s degree or comparable qualification that meets BI’s PhD entry requirements . The qualifying degree must include thesis work, normally 15–30 ECTS, and applicants must have a grade point average of B or higher, equivalent to 4 on BI’s five-point scale. An MBA does not qualify for admission. Relevant backgrounds include law, socio-legal studies, political science, public administration, science and technology studies, economics, or related social science and interdisciplinary fields. Knowledge of technology regulation, EU or administrative law, or digital governance would be an advantage, as would an interest in regulatory reform and how institutions evaluate, revise and retire rules. Experience with legal analysis, case studies, interviews, or qualitative and quantitative analysis is welcome. You are not expected to have experience in all these areas. We are looking for strong analytical and academic writing skills, the ability to work independently and systematically, and a willingness to collaborate across disciplines. Excellent written and spoken English and fulfilment of the MSCA eligibility conditions below are required. Employment and support The appointment is a full-time, four-year position based in Oslo. The annual salary is currently NOK 623,200 gross and will be adjusted in accordance with applicable regulations. The position normally offers membership in BI’s collective pension scheme. Twenty-five per cent of working time is allocated to teaching or other career-promoting duties; 36 research months are funded through REGULAIRE. MSCA funding also provides support for mobility and, where applicable, family allowances under the grant rules. BI will confirm the applicable arrangements as part of the employment terms. You will have access to the network’s research, training and networking activities. MSCA eligibility Applicants may be of any nationality. On the first day of MSCA employment, the selected candidate must meet the following conditions: Not hold a doctoral degree. Applicants who have successfully defended a doctoral thesis are ineligible even if the degree has not yet been formally awarded. Not have resided or carried out their main activity, such as work or study, in Norway for more than 12 months during the preceding 36 months. Compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent in a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention are excluded. Be enrolled in, or meet the conditions for timely enrolment in, BI’s doctoral programme. Appointment is conditional on documentary verification of these conditions and fulfilment of BI’s admission requirements. See the official MSCA Doctoral Networks guidance for further information. How to apply Submit your application through the “Send application” button on this vacancy’s page in BI’s electronic recruitment system. Please consult BI’s PhD application guidance and upload the following: A cover letter explaining your motivation and relevant qualifications, identifying REGULAIRE DC08. A CV, including publications and other relevant research activities, where applicable. An original five-page project proposal addressing this doctoral project, including the research topic, proposed methods, schedule and relevant literature. Any use of generative AI to produce text must be clearly stated and cited. Bachelor’s and master’s degree certificates and transcripts, with a Diploma Supplement or equivalent explanation of the study and grading system and eligibility for further study. Include other relevant certificates and transcripts. At least two recommendation letters from relevant employers, professors or supervisors providing evidence of your research potential. BI’s signed verification document . Documentation of English proficiency, or an English test waiver application with supporting evidence, where required. Certificates and the course approval form for previously completed doctoral courses, if you wish to request recognition of these courses. Applicants still completing their master’s degree should upload their latest transcript and state their expected completion date. The qualifying degree must be completed before the appointment begins. All application documents must be submitted through the electronic system, not by email. Documents received after the deadline will not be considered, except for the final master’s certificate where the degree is being completed shortly before the agreed start date. Applications cannot be revised after submission. Candidates may be asked for further information or invited to an interview. English language requirements BI normally accepts native English speakers, applicants with a qualifying master’s degree from a Nordic institution, and applicants whose qualifying master’s degree was taught in English as meeting its English-language requirement. Other applicants must normally provide TOEFL or IELTS results, or apply for a waiver. BI’s published minimum is IELTS 6.5 or TOEFL iBT 90 on the 0–120 scale, with results from the preceding two years. Consult BI’s PhD entry requirements for current score requirements, accepted documentation and reporting instructions. TOEFL results should be sent directly to BI using institution code 0529; IELTS copies are accepted when the TRF number is visible. GMAT or GRE may be requested in particular cases; BI will inform applicants if required. Contact For questions about REGULAIRE or this doctoral project, contact Professor Sebastian Schwemer at sebastian.f.schwemer@bi.no . For questions about the PhD specialisation in Communication, contact Associate Dean Professor Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no . For questions about admission requirements and the application process, contact BI’s PhD Administration at phd@bi.no . Further information about the network is available at REGULAIRE . REGULAIRE is funded by the European Union under the Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions, Grant Agreement No. 101312218. Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 15/11/2026 Expected start date: 01/01/2027 Location: Campus Oslo Company homepage: https://www.bi.no/ Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3666267/200453?iso=no": {
      "title": "PhD scholarship in Outsider Innovation as Foresight Infrastructure",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad  - PhD scholarship in Outsider Innovation as Foresight Infrastructure PhD scholarship in Outsider Innovation as Foresight Infrastructure Send application BI Norwegian Business School’s Department of Communication and Culture and Department of Law and Governance invite applications for a four-year doctoral scholarship focusing on outsider innovation, early-warning signals and regulatory foresight for transformative technologies. The expected start date is 1 January 2027, or upon agreement. Position details Location: BI Norwegian Business School, Oslo, Norway Position: One PhD candidate position, full time, fixed-term contract of 48 months Doctoral programme: PhD specialisation in Communication at BI Norwegian Business School Application deadline: 15 November 2026 at 23:59 CET Expected start: 1 January 2027, or upon agreement About REGULAIRE The scholarship is part of REGULAIRE (Regulatory Learning for the Governance of Transformative Technologies), a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network funded by the European Union under Horizon Europe. Coordinated by Professor Urs Gasser at the Technical University of Munich, with Professor Sebastian Schwemer as principal investigator at BI, the network brings together 15 doctoral candidates at ten European universities. REGULAIRE studies how regulation can learn to govern transformative technologies such as AI, quantum computing and neurotechnology. Its projects examine how regulators recognise emerging problems, test interventions, develop institutional capacities and share lessons across organisations and jurisdictions. You will join an international cohort with supervision across institutions, joint training and collaboration with regulators, standardisation bodies and international organisations. The doctoral project Open-source communities, civic technologists, small and medium-sized enterprises and marginalised user groups often notice the risks and possibilities of new technologies before regulators do. This doctoral project, “Outside-In Signals: Outsider Innovation as Foresight Infrastructure”, examines how these actors generate early insights, why institutions sometimes overlook them, and how their knowledge can inform regulatory foresight. You will investigate where outsider innovators sit in networks and geographical settings, how signals emerge through sources such as issue trackers and incident reports, and how authorities assess their relevance. Possible approaches include case studies, interviews, document analysis and network analysis. The project also involves developing and testing routines through which authorities can receive, assess and escalate outsider inputs while preserving legality and proportionality. The research is expected to produce a typology of outsider signals, a curated dataset and codebook, and tested procedures for translating signals into regulatory review. It will also develop guidance on maintaining channels through which outsider knowledge can inform ongoing foresight. The successful candidate will develop an independent PhD project within this theme and contribute to REGULAIRE’s interdisciplinary research agenda. Research environment and supervision You will be based in BI’s Department of Communication and Culture and also affiliated with the Department of Law and Governance. Communication and Culture is an international research environment working on communication technologies, online communities, digital transformation and the social legitimacy of technological change. Law and Governance contributes expertise in national and European regulation of markets and technology, digital governance and public policy. You will also join BI’s Nordic Centre for Internet and Society (NCIS), an interdisciplinary research centre studying how emerging internet technologies affect working life and society. Your main supervisor will be Professor Christian Fieseler (BI), with Professor Sebastian Schwemer as co-supervisor at BI and Sandra Cortesi at the Technical University of Munich as the international academic co-supervisor. A sectoral mentor will support the secondment. You will meet your main supervisor at least monthly and the full supervisory team at least four times a year. Your progress and training will be recorded in a personal Career Development Plan. Training and secondment Network training includes three residential summer schools, covering regulatory learning and foresight at TUM, methods and analytics at BI, and academic writing and impact at the University of St. Gallen. You will also take part in methods workshops, monthly paper development seminars and annual network reviews. This training complements the coursework and other requirements of BI’s doctoral programme. The project includes a planned three-month intersectoral secondment, providing opportunities to work with foresight practitioners on emerging technological signals and ways to bring outsider insights into regulatory decisions. The host, timing and detailed arrangements will be confirmed with the supervisory team. Your work Refine the research questions and design with the supervisory team. Collect, manage and analyse research material using methods appropriate to the project. Complete a doctoral thesis and related research publications, and communicate findings to academic and practitioner audiences. Participate in local and network training, cohort activities, reviews and practitioner engagement, including the summer schools and planned secondment. Contribute to teaching or other career-promoting duties as part of the four-year appointment. Your profile Applicants must hold, or complete by the start date, a relevant two-year master’s degree or comparable qualification that meets BI’s PhD entry requirements . The qualifying degree must include thesis work, normally 15–30 ECTS, and applicants must have a grade point average of B or higher, equivalent to 4 on BI’s five-point scale. An MBA does not qualify for admission. Relevant backgrounds include communication and media studies, innovation studies, science and technology studies, information systems, sociology, political science, law, public administration, or related interdisciplinary fields. An interest in outsider innovation, open-source communities, civic technology, or how regulators identify and assess emerging technological risks would be an advantage. Relevant experience may include foresight, digital governance, risk communication, interviews, case studies, document analysis, network analysis, or other qualitative and quantitative methods. You are not expected to have experience in all these areas. We are looking for strong analytical and academic writing skills, the ability to work independently and systematically, and a willingness to collaborate across disciplines. Excellent written and spoken English and fulfilment of the MSCA eligibility conditions below are required. Employment and support The appointment is a full-time, four-year position based in Oslo. The annual salary is currently NOK 623,200 gross and will be adjusted in accordance with applicable regulations. The position normally offers membership in BI’s collective pension scheme. Twenty-five per cent of working time is allocated to teaching or other career-promoting duties; 36 research months are funded through REGULAIRE. MSCA funding also provides support for mobility and, where applicable, family allowances under the grant rules. BI will confirm the applicable arrangements as part of the employment terms. You will have access to the network’s research, training and networking activities. MSCA eligibility Applicants may be of any nationality. On the first day of MSCA employment, the selected candidate must meet the following conditions: Not hold a doctoral degree. Applicants who have successfully defended a doctoral thesis are ineligible even if the degree has not yet been formally awarded. Not have resided or carried out their main activity, such as work or study, in Norway for more than 12 months during the preceding 36 months. Compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent in a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention are excluded. Be enrolled in, or meet the conditions for timely enrolment in, BI’s doctoral programme. Appointment is conditional on documentary verification of these conditions and fulfilment of BI’s admission requirements. See the official MSCA Doctoral Networks guidance for further information. How to apply Submit your application through the “Send application” button on this vacancy’s page in BI’s electronic recruitment system. Please consult BI’s PhD application guidance and upload the following: A cover letter explaining your motivation and relevant qualifications, identifying REGULAIRE DC03. A CV, including publications and other relevant research activities, where applicable. An original five-page project proposal addressing this doctoral project, including the research topic, proposed methods, schedule and relevant literature. Any use of generative AI to produce text must be clearly stated and cited. Bachelor’s and master’s degree certificates and transcripts, with a Diploma Supplement or equivalent explanation of the study and grading system and eligibility for further study. Include other relevant certificates and transcripts. At least two recommendation letters from relevant employers, professors or supervisors providing evidence of your research potential. BI’s signed verification document . Documentation of English proficiency, or an English test waiver application with supporting evidence, where required. Certificates and the course approval form for previously completed doctoral courses, if you wish to request recognition of these courses. Applicants still completing their master’s degree should upload their latest transcript and state their expected completion date. The qualifying degree must be completed before the appointment begins. All application documents must be submitted through the electronic system, not by email. Documents received after the deadline will not be considered, except for the final master’s certificate where the degree is being completed shortly before the agreed start date. Applications cannot be revised after submission. Candidates may be asked for further information or invited to an interview. English language requirements BI normally accepts native English speakers, applicants with a qualifying master’s degree from a Nordic institution, and applicants whose qualifying master’s degree was taught in English as meeting its English-language requirement. Other applicants must normally provide TOEFL or IELTS results, or apply for a waiver. BI’s published minimum is IELTS 6.5 or TOEFL iBT 90 on the 0–120 scale, with results from the preceding two years. Consult BI’s PhD entry requirements for current score requirements, accepted documentation and reporting instructions. TOEFL results should be sent directly to BI using institution code 0529; IELTS copies are accepted when the TRF number is visible. GMAT or GRE may be requested in particular cases; BI will inform applicants if required. Contact For questions about this doctoral project or the PhD specialisation in Communication, contact Professor Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no . For questions about REGULAIRE at BI, contact Professor Sebastian Schwemer at sebastian.f.schwemer@bi.no . For questions about admission requirements and the application process, contact BI’s PhD Administration at phd@bi.no . Further information about the network is available at REGULAIRE . REGULAIRE is funded by the European Union under the Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions, Grant Agreement No. 101312218. Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 15/11/2026 Expected start date: 01/01/2027 Location: Campus Oslo Company homepage: https://www.bi.no/ Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
