    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3665567/45436?iso=no": {
      "title": "Associate Professor / Professor in Organizational Behaviour",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Campus Trondheim - Associate Professor / Professor in Organizational Behaviour Associate Professor / Professor in Organizational Behaviour Send application Associate Professor / Professor in Organizational Behaviour BI Norwegian Business School invites applications for two positions as Associate Professor or Professor in Organizational Behaviour at Campus Trondheim. BI Norwegian Business School is one of Scandinavia's leading business schools, known for combining research-based knowledge with strong ties to business and industry. Campus Trondheim sits in one of Norway's most attractive student cities — a place consistently rated among the best student destinations in the country, with a vibrant academic environment and a close-knit local business community. Today, the campus hosts eight Norwegian bachelor's degree programmes with a total of 2,200 students across three academic years.\" The position will be organizationally affiliated with Campus Trondheim, while also being part of the Department of Leadership and Organization at the Bergen, Stavanger and Oslo campus. This gives you both a local academic home and a broader national research community to be part of. Campus Trondheim is establishing a new Master programme in leadership and organizational psychology set to start in autumn 2027 and is now building the academic community needed to get there. These two positions are a central part of that build-up and mark the start of growing local capacity in leadership and organizational science, alongside related fields such as research methodology and organizational ethics and sustainability. Job description BI has an ambition to make its mark in the international research community while at the same time developing our students’ academic competence and skills. Relevance and impact on practice are ensured through extensive contact with business and industry. Teaching must be research based, show practical relevance and and develop students' academic and practical competence. Expectations and obligations Our faculty is expected to provide teaching and supervision of a high academic and pedagogical quality and is expected to continuously develop their teaching skills, e.g. by making use of new technology, new ways of learning and new forms of assessment. We have focus on quality and competence development and pedagogical activities should be continuously documented, supporting the process for further academic career development to all positions. For all positions with research duties BI has high expectations for research production. Failing to meet the expectations could lead to increased duties in teaching. Those employed in a scientific position will also have teaching and pedagogical development responsibilities. In addition to traditional teaching, the position includes development of courses, learning activities, forms of evaluation, and guidance. Qualification Requirements Required • A PhD in organizational behaviour, organizational psychology, management, or a closely related discipline • An established track record in top-quality outlets within the field, matching the level required for Associate Professor or Professor as applicable • Fulfilment of BI's requirements for educational qualifications for academic staff, in accordance with governmental provisions and the relevant scientific level • Proficiency in English, both written and oral. As the position may involve teaching in Norwegian, Norwegian language proficiency at a minimum of B2 level is required or must be attained within three years of appointment. Desirable • Research expertise in one or more of the following areas: leadership and organizational science, quantitative research methodology, or ethics, sustainability and organizational culture • Documented teaching experience • Experience with interactive or process-oriented teaching methods • International research experience within business, management, or organizational psychology • Experience contributing to the development of study programmes or new courses According to governmental provisions BI has decided on requirements concerning the educational qualifications of permanently employed academic staff. As a minimum such qualifications shall meet the learning outcome of BI ‘s pedagogical basic qualification courses or equivalent at all times. To the position of Lecturer and Associate Professor, and in some rare instances for international professors, this specific competence may be obtained within two years after commencing the position. Personal qualities We are looking for someone who: • Is genuinely motivated to build and grow an academic community, not just join an established one • Communicates clearly and engages well with students, colleagues, and external partners • Takes initiative and is comfortable working in a developing environment where structures are still taking shape • Balances academic rigor with practical relevance What we can offer • The opportunity to shape a growing academic environment from an early stage • Membership in BI's collective pension scheme • A strong professional environment with good research support and publication incentives • A clear focus on career development, both in research and teaching • A vibrant, attractive city to live and work in, with strong support services for staff and — where relevant — their families • Relocation support Application procedure Qualifications will be evaluated according to the usual rules for academic positions at universities and specialized university institutions, as well as BI provisions for appointment and promotion. Candidates interested in this position must submit their application electronically through the “Send application” button, and upload the following documents: • Cover letter • CV and publication list • Certificate from the highest degree obtained • Scientific works and pedagogical dissemination articles (up to 15 works) • An educational portfolio including the following items: - An \"educational CV\", which provides a concise description of your experience in planning, implementation and evaluation and development of teaching and supervision, including scope and level - A reflection on your own role as a teacher, and a discussion and justification your own choices in planning, implementation and development of teaching and supervision - A documentation of completed as educational competence courses. • Other documentation that may be important for assessing the level of competence Work may not be submitted after the deadline. Qualified applicants may be called in for an interview, trial lecture or seminar presentation. BI Norwegian Business School reserves the right not to appoint applicants who are academically qualified if they are found not to satisfy other explicit requirements with respect to pedagogical skills, teaching experience, work experience in or in cooperation with the business sector or inter-personal skills. Further information For further information regarding the recruitment process, documentation requirements, and evaluation criteria, please see this guide Further information about the position can be obtained by contacting Thorvald Hærem, Head of Department, +47 46410720/ thorvald.harem@bi.no or Asgeir Opland, Director Campus Trondheim, +4793201655/ asgeir.opland@bi.no. For more information about the department and the school visit https://www.bi.no/en/. About Trondheim For more information on Trondheim, please visit this this site Print Send application Share on: Region: Trøndelag Application deadline: 01.11.2026 Expected start date: Etter avtale Location: Trondheim Contacts: Thorvald Hærem Mobile: +47 46410720 Company homepage: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Campus Trondheim BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3659235/45436?iso=no": {
      "title": "Associate Professor / Professor",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for ledelse og organisasjon - Department of Leadership and Organizational Behaviour  - Associate Professor / Professor Associate Professor / Professor Søk her Associate Professor/ Professor in Organizational Behaviour BI Norwegian Business School invites applications for the position of Professor/Associate Professor in Organizational Behaviour at the Department of Leadership and Organizational Behaviour. The Department has a strong focus on the interaction between research and teaching, and for the employees of the department this means that they focus both on publishing in top academic journals as well as being able to communicate this in their teaching. The department is divided into three faculty groups: • Leadership, Change and Sustainability • Project Management • Organizational Psychology The teaching delivered by the department faculty covers all academic levels, and we offer courses on most of BI’s study programs, including Executive Master of Management, MSc, EMBA, PhD and Bachelor of Management. Job description Through its academic work, BI has an ambition to make its mark in the international research community while at the same time developing our students’ academic competence and skills. Relevance and impact on practice will be ensured through extensive contact with business and industry. Teaching must be based on the cutting edge of research, show practical relevance for working life and develop both academic competence and skills in the students. The Department has recently established the BI Center for Leadership Development (CLD), and we are looking for a candidate who can actively contribute to the center’s vision and research agenda. CLD aims to bridge the gap between academia and practice through innovative research on leader training, interventions, and evaluation. We seek a colleague whose expertise—particularly within leadership development, quantitative evaluations, or engaged scholarship—can strengthen the center’s ongoing projects and its collaboration with external industry partners and our own executive programs. The successful candidate holds a degree in organizational psychology. The candidate should have expertise in the psychosocial work environment, leadership, career development, and diversity, equity, and inclusion in working life. In “line with BI's guidelines, candidates lacking sufficient pedagogical competence must acquire this within two years of appointment. Competence in quantitative research methods, particularly longitudinal research designs and analysis, is considered an advantage. Much of the thesis advice that the department provides requires quantitative competency, and we are therefore seeking candidates with knowledge in quantitative methods. Expectations and obligations Our faculty is expected to provide teaching and supervision of a high academic and pedagogical quality and is expected to continuously develop their teaching skills, e.g. by making use of new technology, new ways of learning and new forms of assessment. We have focus on quality and competence development and pedagogical activities should be continuously documented, supporting the process for further academic career development to all positions. For all positions with research duties BI have high expectations for research production. Failing to meet the expectations could lead to increased duties in teaching. Those employed in a scientific position will also have teaching and pedagogical development responsibilities. In addition to traditional teaching, the position includes development of courses, learning activities, forms of evaluation, and guidance. Qualification Requirements Scientific positions • PhD degree, discipline, (and if relevant desired institutions/international Ph.D) • A (significant) academic production considerably above and beyond a Ph.D is required. • BI requirements concerning educational qualifications according to governmental provisions and the specific scientific level. Other assessment criteria • Language skills; English written and oral (Norwegian if relevant). All pedagogical and research positions must document language proficiency, according to Norwegian law, in Norwegian at minimum the B2 level or be subject to acquiring the level within 3 years of the appointment. • Good communication skills The following qualifications will be also considered in the assessment of the applicants: • Documented experience with interactive and process-oriented teaching methods. • Knowledge of and interest in modern pedagogical approaches, as well as the development of study programs, will be appreciated. • Weight will also be given to experience from public sector, business and/or the public sector, including R&D work, research dissemination and research-based advisory services. • International work experience in the field of economics and business administration is valued. According to governmental provisions BI has decided on requirements concerning the educational qualifications of permanently employed academic staff. As a minimum such qualifications shall meet the learning outcome of BI‘s pedagogical basic qualification courses or equivalent at all times. To the position of Lecturer and Associate Professor, and in some rare instances for international professors, this specific competence may be obtained within two years after commencing the position. What we can offer • The position normally offers membership in BIs collective pension scheme. • A good working environment at one of Scandinavia's leading business schools. • Good research support and publication remuneration. • Supportive professional environment. • Strong focus on professional career development. Application procedure Qualifications will be evaluated according to the usual rules for academic positions at universities and specialized university institutions, as well as BI provisions for appointment and promotion. Candidates interested in this position must submit their application electronically through the “Send application” button, and upload the following documents: • Cover letter • CV and publication list • Certificate from the highest degree obtained • Scientific works and pedagogical dissemination articles (up to 15 works) • An educational portfolio including the following items: · An \"educational CV\", which provides a concise description of your experience in planning, implementation and evaluation and development of teaching and supervision, including scope and level · Be able to reflect on your own role as a teacher, and discuss and justify your own choices in planning, implementation and development of teaching and supervision · Documentation of cited conditions as educational competence course • Other documentation that may be important for assessing the level of competence Work may not be submitted after the deadline. BI Norwegian Business School is an equal opportunity employer. Qualified applicants may be called in for an interview, trial lecture or seminar presentation. BI Norwegian Business School reserves the right not to appoint applicants who are academically qualified if they are found not to satisfy other explicit requirements with respect to pedagogical skills, teaching experience, work experience in or in cooperation with the business sector or inter-personal skills. Further information For further information regarding the recruitment process, documentation requirements, and evaluation criteria, please see this guide Further information about the position can be obtained by contacting Thorvald Hærem, Head of Department, telephone 46410720, Thorvald.harem@bi.no. For more information about the department and the school visit: Institutt for ledelse og organisasjon | BI About Oslo Oslo, the capital of Norway, is an international, fast growing, and attractive city. For more information on Oslo, please visit this site Skriv ut Søk her Del på: Fylke: Oslo Jobbtype: Fast Heltid/Deltid: Heltid Arbeidstid: Dag Søknadsfrist: 15.10.2026 Tiltredelse: Etter avtale Arbeidssted: Oslo Kontaktpersoner: Thorvald Hærem mob: +47 46410720 Hjemmeside: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for ledelse og organisasjon - Department of Leadership and Organizational Behaviour The Department of Leadership and Organizational Behavior is a profiled research department at BI and produced almost one third of the institution’s publications in 2018, where a significant number of these was published in highly ranked journals. The department has the ambition to further increase its impact both nationally and internationally. Active research areas include HRM topics like motivational processes and effectiveness, knowledge sharing, employee development, empowerment etc, leadership issues, change management, creativity, project management and leadership, decision making, organizational complexity, individual differences in personality, cognitive style, and abilities, knowledge management and more. The Department is responsible for courses at all levels (Bachelor, Master of Science, PhD and Executive Programs) in Norway and abroad in the fields of Organizational Behavior, Leadership, HRM, Change management, and more. The school offers attractive opportunities in corporate teaching. The Department is currently home to about 39 full-time academic faculty, 10 PhD students. Tilbake til toppen"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3664851/200453?iso=no": {
      "title": "PhD in Economics BI Norwegian Business School invites applications for a four-year PhD position in Economics PhD in Economics",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad  - PhD in Economics BI Norwegian Business School invites applications for a four-year PhD position in Economics PhD in Economics Send application BI Norwegian Business School is one of Europe's largest educational institutions, with over 20.000 students. As Norway's leading business school, BI builds on a unique tradition of combining academic rigour and professional relevance. Our programmes range from bachelor's to master's and PhD programmes. BI is also known for its many customized executive education programmes. BI boasts some of Europe's leading academic departments within the fields of economics, finance, strategy, marketing, management and data science. As an employee at BI Norwegian Business School you will meet exciting challenges in an engaging and international environment, with over 800 colleagues who continuously push the frontiers of knowledge. PhD Candidates in Economics BI Norwegian Business School offers a four-year Ph.D. Programme with specialization in Economics, which trains students to engage in high quality scientific research, making use of economic theory and state of the art econometric methods. The Department of Economics has a strong research-oriented faculty working within microeconomic theory, macroeconomics, econometrics, labour economics, natural resource and energy economics, political economics, industrial organization, and international and development economics. The department hosts five research centres; Centre for Monetary Economics (CME), Centre for Research in Economics and Management (CREAM), Centre for Applied Macro and Petroleum Economics (CAMP), Centre for Experimental Studies and Research (CESAR), and Centre for Applied and Theoretical Econometrics (CATE). The doctoral programme is an important element in the department's research excellence strategy and in BI's vision to become one of the leading research business schools in Europe. The selected candidates will join a group of talented and ambitious PhD candidates at the department that work in close collaboration with faculty members. PhD students are expected to participate actively in research seminars and workshops. The place of work will be at BI Norwegian Business School in Oslo. A PhD from BI can be a gateway to prestigious academic positions at universities and research institutions around the world. We encourage and support our candidates in the international job market. Recent graduates of BI's PhD specialization in economics have been hired by, among others, Sciences Po, University of Chicago, Harvard Business School, Católica Lisbon School of Business & Economics, King’s College London, University of Oslo and Research Division of Norges Bank . Eligible applicants must have completed a Master of Science degree with thesis work, a Research Masters/M.Phil degree, or a comparable degree relevant for the specialization in Economics by the time the programme starts in early autumn of 2027, with a grade point average of B or higher. An MBA degree does not fulfill the requirements for admission. The four-year scholarship is set according to the Norwegian State Salary Scale, and currently pays NOK 623,200 (this will be adjusted). The position normally offers membership in BI's collective pension scheme. The scholarship involves 25% work obligation. BI Norwegian Business School is an equal opportunity employer. We encourage women to apply for the positions. Applications must be submitted electronically by using the \"Send Application\" button on the right-hand side of this announcement. Paper or e-mail applications will not be accepted. You are required to submit the following electronically together with your application: Background Information: A short personal statement describing your research interests and potential research projects. Please also include BI Department of Economics faculty members that you may be interested in working closely with should you be admitted to the PhD program. The list of our faculty members can be found here . Curriculum Vitae (CV). We do not require full research proposals for concrete projects (while applications to other departments may do). Letters of Recommendation: We require a minimum of two (preferably three) letters of recommendation from people that can comment on your research potential. These should be emailed directly to us by the letter writers rather than uploaded by you. However, you must include the names of your letter writers in your application. Please arrange with the people submitting recommendation letters to email their recommendations to phd.economics@bi.no by the application deadline . Academic Transcripts: Certificate of a Master of Science degree or equivalent, including transcript of grades. Certificate of a Bachelor degree or equivalent, including transcript of grades. A Diploma Supplement or a similar document describing in detail the study and grade system associated with the obtained degree. Signed transcript verification document ( to be found here ) . (Please note that this document should be uploaded with your application and not sent to your university. We will use the document to request an official transcript if you are considered for admission to the program and check for any discrepancy with the transcript you uploaded.) Test Scores: GRE General/GMAT test score: All applicants, except those who hold a master's degree from an institution inside the European Economic Area/the European Union or Switzerland, must submit results from GMAT or the GRE General test. Note that we need scores for the Verbal, Quantitative and Analytical Writing sections of the GRE General or GMAT to consider your application. Incomplete sections or partial scores are not sufficient to consider your application. TOEFL/IELTS test score: Applicants whose native language is not English must take either TOEFL or IELTS. To be considered for admission, applicants must have a minimum TOEFL score of 575 (written test)/233 (computer-based test)/90 (internet-based test) or a IELTS score of 6.5. In some cases, applicants can request a waiver from TOEFL/IELTS by submitting a waiver form ( found here ) together with the application. As a general rule, exemptions are made for applicants: who have two years or more of higher education where the language of instruction was English. from a country which is a member of the EU/EEA and/or the Council of Europe/UNESCO-Cepes who have English as the first foreign language taken over a period of at least 7 years in primary school, secondary school and upper secondary education. We accept scanned copies of test scores. TOEFL/IELTS scores must be from within the last two years. For scanned IELTS scores, the TRF number must be visible. In case of sending official score reports from the test center, you can use the following codes for BI Norwegian Business School: TOEFL/GMAT: 0529. GRE: 7365. Additional documents: In addition, you may upload the following documents: A writing sample that documents relevant research activities (e.g. a Master's thesis or academic paper). Certificates from previously completed courses at the doctoral level, if requesting approval of these for the PhD programme at BI Norwegian Business School (see section 2 in our PhD Regulations). If you have taken any courses at PhD level please complete the following form (maximum 18 ECTS). All documents, apart from the recommendation letters, must be uploaded in the electronic application and may not be submitted by e-mail or on paper. Furthermore, note that any documents forwarded after the application deadline will not be considered. Exceptions will only be made for students who are completing their master's degree in 2026 and do not have a final certificate when applying. Please note that you will not be able to revise your application once it has been submitted. Candidates may, at a later stage, be asked to forward further information and/or be called in for an interview. Candidates who are admitted to the programme must provide a more detailed progress plan with milestones and courses for the four years of study. All documentation that is requested is listed in this announcement text. Please read the announcement text carefully. If you have additional questions, that are not covered by the text, you can contact info@bi.no or the academic department. Please note that at BI we are unable to pre-assess qualifications, convert grades, or provide guidance on how to strengthen an application before it is submitted. Applications are assessed by the relevant PhD Evaluation Committee as part of the formal recruitment process. Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 15/01/2027 Expected start date: 16/08/2027 Location: Campus Oslo Company homepage: https://www.bi.no/ Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3663827/200453?iso=no": {
      "title": "EMANAIRE project: Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad  - PhD Specialisation in Communication - EMANAIRE project: Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project: “Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI” PhD Specialisation in Communication - EMANAIRE project: Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI Send application BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI”. About EMANAIRE EMANAIRE: https://emanaire.eu/ is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network bringing together 15 doctoral candidates at nine European universities to study how AI is changing work. Its projects examine what happens when generative and agentic AI enters leadership, everyday workflows, team communication, professional learning and career decisions. Across the network, human agency means the practical ability to understand AI-supported decisions, exercise judgement, question outputs and shape how work is organised. Each researcher joins an international cohort, receives supervision across institutions and takes part in three residential schools, methods workshops, monthly research seminars and joint work with organisations beyond academia. Every project includes a planned six-month intersectoral secondment. The doctoral project Two employees can encounter the same generative AI roll-out and see very different futures. One may find new ways to use their skills; another may fear redundancy, lose confidence or see no credible path to reskilling. Generative AI is changing what many jobs consist of and which skills are valued in them, and the consequences for individual careers are uneven and hard to predict. Employees face open questions about which parts of their work will remain theirs and whether they can realistically acquire the skills that will matter next. Organisations, in turn, need to understand why the same technology is experienced as an opportunity by some employees and as a threat by others, and what they can do so that adjustment does not depend on individual luck or resources. This doctoral project studies how employees make sense of generative AI in relation to their own careers, and how the conditions they work under shape what follows from that assessment. It asks how career adaptability, supervisory and organisational support, opportunities to use and develop skills, and job satisfaction and engagement help explain why some employees come through AI-driven change with sustainable careers while others lose ground. Reskilling and internal mobility receive particular attention as routes to sustainable career outcomes, together with the forms of support and communication that make such routes credible to employees. The doctorate will produce evidence on the patterns of risk and resilience that emerge as generative AI enters workplaces, and on the organisational conditions that help employees retain agency over their own careers. Its academic contribution lies in explaining why similar technological changes lead to different career trajectories across employees and workplaces. It should also result in practical guidance that helps organisations recognise where employees feel confident, constrained or anxious and respond with appropriate support and communication during AI-driven change. The specific research questions and design are not fixed in advance and will be developed together with the supervisory team. Applicants are encouraged to bring their own ideas to the project proposal that forms part of the application. Your work Examine how employees appraise generative AI as a threat or an opportunity for their own careers, and how these appraisals relate to career adaptability, skill use, job satisfaction and engagement. Study how supervisory and organisational support shape risk and resilience during AI-driven change, and when reskilling and internal mobility lead to sustainable career outcomes. Identify the conditions under which employees retain agency over their careers as generative AI changes the content of their work. Translate the findings into guidance for organisations on supporting employees and communicating during AI-driven change. Refine the research questions and design with the supervision team and prepare ethics, data-management and open-science materials. Complete a doctoral thesis and related research outputs under the host doctoral programme and EMANAIRE plan. Take part in local and network-wide training, cohort activities, reviews, dissemination and practitioner engagement. Undertake the six-month intersectoral secondment and the separate academic mobility visit. Contribute to research integrity, equality, responsible AI, open science and FAIR data practices. Who we are looking for The candidate will join BI's four-year doctoral programme, designed for talented students who wish to pursue an international academic career and contribute to the international research community through high-quality publications. Recruitment of PhD candidates is an important part of the department's research agenda and BI's ambition to be a leading European business school. Eligible applicants must have completed, or complete by the start date, a Master of Science degree with thesis work, a research master's/M.Phil. degree, or a comparable degree relevant to the PhD specialisation in Communication. Relevant disciplinary backgrounds include, but are not limited to, communication, media studies, human-computer interaction, information systems, sociology, psychology, management, organisational behaviour, human resource management, law and technology, science and technology studies, political science, or related social-science and interdisciplinary fields. An MBA degree does not fulfil the requirements for admission. For this project, the following qualities will be viewed as advantageous: Interest in careers, HRM, organisational or work psychology, reskilling or generative AI at work. Strong quantitative or mixed-methods skills and interest in measurement and longitudinal organisational research. Experience with surveys, multivariate analysis, person-centred approaches or data visualisation would be advantageous. Ability to translate employee-level evidence into organisation-level insight and practical guidance. Strong academic writing skills and motivation to publish in international peer-reviewed journals. Supervision and integration at BI The selected candidate will receive close supervision and follow-up from research faculty. The main supervisors are Professor Christian Fieseler and Associate Professor Dominique Kost (BI Norwegian Business School). The candidate will be integrated into the Department of Communication and Culture and the wider EMANAIRE network. Employment and support The four-year scholarship is currently NOK 623,200 gross per year and will be adjusted according to applicable regulations. The position normally offers membership in BI's collective pension scheme . Twenty-five per cent of working time is reserved for teaching. The candidate's workplace will be BI Norwegian Business School in Oslo, Norway. Further information about the doctoral programme and admission requirements is available from BI's PhD Administration (phd@bi.no) and the PhD specialisation in Communication: https://www.bi.no/en/programmes-and-individual-courses/phd/communication/. How to apply Candidates are asked to upload the following material with their electronic application: Certificate of a Master degree (MSc, MA, or equivalent) , including transcripts of grades. The qualifying degree must include thesis work and be completed by the start date, with a grade point average of B or higher. An MBA degree does not meet the admission requirements. Certificate of a bachelor's degree or equivalent, including transcripts of grades. BI requires a two-year master's programme and average grades of B or higher for eligibility. A Diploma Supplement , or a comparable document explaining the study and grading system and the rights for further study associated with the degree. Other relevant certificates and transcripts. A 2,000-word original project proposal that engages independently with this doctoral project, including the proposed research focus, methods, schedule and relevant literature. Any use of generative AI to produce text must be clearly stated and cited. Curriculum vitae. At least two letters of recommendation from relevant employers, professors or supervisors that provide evidence of the applicant's research potential. Certificates from previously completed doctoral-level courses, if requesting approval of these courses for the BI PhD programme at BI Norwegian Business School. If you have taken any courses at PhD level please complete the following form (maximum 18 ECTS). A complete list of publications and other documented relevant activities, where applicable. Signed Verification Document Applicants who do not hold a qualifying master's degree from a Nordic institution and whose qualifying degree was not taught in English must normally document English proficiency through TOEFL or IELTS, unless a waiver is granted. The minimum accepted scores are: TOEFL: 575 on the written test, 233 on the computer-based test, or 90 on the internet-based test. IELTS: overall score of 6.5. TOEFL and IELTS scores must be from the preceding two years. The BI institution codes are GMAT 0529, TOEFL 0529 and GRE 7365. IELTS copies are accepted when the TRF number is visible. Applicants who believe they qualify for a waiver should submit the required waiver documentation. Please note that all documents must be uploaded in the electronic application system. Documents may not be submitted by e-mail. Furthermore, documents submitted after the application deadline will not be considered. Exceptions will only be made for students who are completing their master’s degree in 2026 and do not have a final certificate when applying. Please note that applicants will not be able to revise their application once it has been submitted. Candidates may later be asked for further information and/or invited to an interview. A candidate admitted to the programme must prepare a more detailed progress plan with milestones and courses for the four years of study. MSCA eligibility Applicants may be of any nationality. To be eligible for MSCA funding, the selected candidate must, on the first day of MSCA employment: (i) not hold a doctoral degree; candidates who have successfully defended a doctoral thesis but have not yet formally received the degree are not eligible; (ii) comply with the MSCA mobility rule, meaning they must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Norway for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding that date, excluding compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent in a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention; and (iii) be enrolled in, or meet the conditions required for timely enrolment in, the doctoral programme leading to the degree specified in this notice. The appointment is conditional on documentary verification of these conditions and fulfilment of the host institution's doctoral admission requirements. More information Questions about the project can be addressed to Professor Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no. Questions about the PhD specialisation in Communication can be addressed to Academic Program Manager Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no. Questions about the application process can be sent to BI's PhD Administration at phd@bi.no. Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 01.11.2026 Expected start date: 01/01/2027 Location: Oslo Contacts: Christian Fieseler Mobile: +47 46410088 Company homepage: https://www.bi.no/ Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3663821/200453?iso=no": {
      "title": "EMANAIRE project: Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad  - PhD specialisation in Communication - EMANAIRE project: Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project: “Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams” PhD specialisation in Communication - EMANAIRE project: Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams Send application BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams”. About EMANAIRE EMANAIRE: https://emanaire.eu/ is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network bringing together 15 doctoral candidates at nine European universities to study how AI is changing work. Its projects examine what happens when generative and agentic AI enters leadership, everyday workflows, team communication, professional learning and career decisions. Across the network, human agency means the practical ability to understand AI-supported decisions, exercise judgement, question outputs and shape how work is organised. Each researcher joins an international cohort, receives supervision across institutions and takes part in three residential schools, methods workshops, monthly research seminars and joint work with organisations beyond academia. Every project includes a planned six-month intersectoral secondment. The doctoral project When a team delegates drafting, analysis or coordination to AI, communication changes even if the organisational chart does not. Colleagues may exchange fewer early ideas, scrutinise outputs less closely or become uncertain about whose judgement shaped the final result. This doctoral project investigates how the outsourcing of essential tasks to AI changes the way leaders and teams communicate and collaborate. Particular attention will be paid to team dynamics, including behavioral aspects such as AI overreliance, disclosure of AI use, and patterns of AI-supported communication; cognitive aspects such as beliefs, assumptions, and mindsets regarding digital teamwork and AI-assisted collaboration; and managerial and personal resources that help employees adapt to changing work demands and maintain critical thinking. The project combines empirical research with scale development and validation to better understand the conditions under which AI-supported collaboration strengthens, rather than undermines, communication quality, team effectiveness, and critical reflection. The doctorate will produce evidence about the communication patterns that emerge in AI-augmented teams and the leadership practices that shape them. It should also deliver validated measures and manager-facing workshops or tools that help teams notice weak scrutiny, protect critical thinking and use delegation without hollowing out collective learning. Findings will be translated into practical tools, workshops, and evidence-based recommendations for managers. The candidate can tailor the research design to some extent: A programme combining measure development with field or team studies is plausible after discussion with the supervisory team about samples, settings, methods and analysis. Your work Examine how delegating work to AI affects leaders' work, team dynamics, AI overreliance and disclosure, implicit theories and mindsets related to digital teams, and critical scrutiny Develop and validate quantitative measures capturing team dynamics in AI assisted teams, and/or leader-employee communication in such settings Identify managerial and team-level resources that help employees meet changing demands from agentic and generative AI and translate the findings into manager-facing workshops and practices. Study whether over-delegation weakens critical scrutiny, skill development and developmental teamwork. Refine the research questions and design with the supervision team and prepare ethics, data-management and open-science materials. Complete a doctoral thesis and related research outputs under the host doctoral programme and EMANAIRE plan. Take part in local and network-wide training, cohort activities, reviews, dissemination and practitioner engagement. Undertake the six-month intersectoral secondment and the separate academic mobility visit. Contribute to research integrity, equality, responsible AI, open science and FAIR data practices. Who we are looking for The candidate will join BI's four-year doctoral programme, designed for talented students who wish to pursue an international academic career and contribute to the international research community through high-quality publications. Recruitment of PhD candidates is an important part of the department's research agenda and BI's ambition to be a leading European business school. Eligible applicants must have completed, or complete by the start date, a Master of Science degree with thesis work, a research master's/M.Phil. degree, or a comparable degree relevant to the PhD specialisation in Communication. Relevant disciplinary backgrounds include, but are not limited to, communication, media studies, human-computer interaction, information systems, sociology, psychology, management, organisational behaviour, human resource management, law and technology, science and technology studies, political science, or related social-science and interdisciplinary fields. An MBA degree does not fulfil the requirements for admission. For this project, the following qualities will be viewed as advantageous: Knowledge of, or a strong interest in, team dynamics, including behavioral aspects such as AI overreliance, disclosure of AI use, and patterns of AI-supported communication, and cognitive patterns regarding digital teamwork and AI-assisted collaboration Strong quantitative or mixed-methods skills, with interest in measurement development and validation. Experience with surveys, experiments, field studies, team research or psychometric analysis would be advantageous. Ability to connect individual, leader and team-level processes in a rigorous research design. Strong academic writing skills and motivation to publish in international peer-reviewed journals. Interest in connecting research and practice through intensive collaboration with the secondment partner Supervision and integration at BI The selected candidate will receive close supervision and follow-up from research faculty. The main supervisors are Professors Suzanne van Gils and Sut I Wong (BI Norwegian Business School). The candidate will be integrated into the Department of Communication and Culture and the wider EMANAIRE network. Employment and support The four-year scholarship is currently NOK 623,200 gross per year and will be adjusted according to applicable regulations. The position normally offers membership in BI's collective pension scheme. Twenty-five per cent of working time is reserved for teaching. The candidate's workplace will be BI Norwegian Business School in Oslo, Norway. Further information about the doctoral programme and admission requirements is available from BI's PhD Administration (phd@bi.no) and the PhD specialisation in Communication: https://www.bi.no/en/programmes-and-individual-courses/phd/communication/. How to apply Candidates are asked to upload the following material with their electronic application: Certificate of a Master degree (MSc, MA, or equivalent) , including transcripts of grades. The qualifying degree must include thesis work and be completed by the start date, with a grade point average of B or higher. An MBA degree does not meet the admission requirements. Certificate of a bachelor's degree or equivalent, including transcripts of grades. BI requires a two-year master's programme and average grades of B or higher for eligibility. A Diploma Supplement , or a comparable document explaining the study and grading system and the rights for further study associated with the degree. Other relevant certificates and transcripts. A 2,000-word original project proposal that engages independently with this doctoral project, including the proposed research focus, methods, schedule and relevant literature. Any use of generative AI to produce text must be clearly stated and cited. Curriculum vitae. At least two letters of recommendation from relevant employers, professors or supervisors that provide evidence of the applicant's research potential. Certificates from previously completed doctoral-level courses, if requesting approval of these courses for the BI PhD programme at BI Norwegian Business School. If you have taken any courses at PhD level please complete the following form (maximum 18 ECTS). A complete list of publications and other documented relevant activities, where applicable. Signed Verification Document Applicants who do not hold a qualifying master's degree from a Nordic institution and whose qualifying degree was not taught in English must normally document English proficiency through TOEFL or IELTS, unless a waiver is granted. The minimum accepted scores are: TOEFL: 575 on the written test, 233 on the computer-based test, or 90 on the internet-based test. IELTS: overall score of 6.5. TOEFL and IELTS scores must be from the preceding two years. The BI institution codes are GMAT 0529, TOEFL 0529 and GRE 7365. IELTS copies are accepted when the TRF number is visible. Applicants who believe they qualify for a waiver should submit the required waiver documentation. Please note that all documents must be uploaded in the electronic application system. Documents may not be submitted by e-mail. Furthermore, documents submitted after the application deadline will not be considered. Exceptions will only be made for students who are completing their master’s degree in 2026 and do not have a final certificate when applying. Please note that applicants will not be able to revise their application once it has been submitted. Candidates may later be asked for further information and/or invited to an interview. A candidate admitted to the programme must prepare a more detailed progress plan with milestones and courses for the four years of study. MSCA eligibility Applicants may be of any nationality. To be eligible for MSCA funding, the selected candidate must, on the first day of MSCA employment: (i) not hold a doctoral degree; candidates who have successfully defended a doctoral thesis but have not yet formally received the degree are not eligible; (ii) comply with the MSCA mobility rule, meaning they must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Norway for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding that date, excluding compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent in a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention; and (iii) be enrolled in, or meet the conditions required for timely enrolment in, the doctoral programme leading to the degree specified in this notice. The appointment is conditional on documentary verification of these conditions and fulfilment of the host institution's doctoral admission requirements. More information Questions about the project can be addressed to Professor Suzanne van Gils at suzanne.v.gils@bi.no. Questions about the PhD specialisation in Communication can be addressed to Academic Program Manager  Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no. Questions about the application process can be sent to BI's PhD Administration at phd@bi.no. Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 01.11.2026 Expected start date: 01/01/2027 Location: Oslo Contacts: Christian Fieseler Mobile: +47 46410088 Company homepage: https://www.bi.no/ Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3663805/200453?iso=no": {
      "title": "PhD specialisation in Communication- EMANAIRE project: Leader Legitimacy in Augmented Leadership BI Norwegian Business School's Departments of Leadership and Organizational Behavior, and Communication and Culture, invite applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Leader Legitimacy in Augmented Leadership” PhD specialisation in Communication- EMANAIRE project: Leader Legitimacy in Augmented Leadership",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad  - PhD specialisation in Communication- EMANAIRE project: Leader Legitimacy in Augmented Leadership BI Norwegian Business School's Departments of Leadership and Organizational Behavior, and Communication and Culture, invite applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Leader Legitimacy in Augmented Leadership” PhD specialisation in Communication- EMANAIRE project: Leader Legitimacy in Augmented Leadership Send application BI Norwegian Business School's Departments of Leadership and Organizational Behavior, and Communication and Culture, invite applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Leader Legitimacy in Augmented Leadership”. About EMANAIRE EMANAIRE: https://emanaire.eu/ is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network bringing together 15 doctoral candidates at nine European universities to study how AI is changing work. Its projects examine what happens when generative and agentic AI enters leadership, everyday workflows, team communication, professional learning and career decisions. Across the network, human agency means the practical ability to understand AI-supported decisions, exercise judgement, question outputs and shape how work is organised. Each researcher joins an international cohort, receives supervision across institutions and takes part in three residential schools, methods workshops, monthly research seminars and joint work with organisations beyond academia. Every project includes a planned six-month intersectoral secondment. The doctoral project AI is giving leadership a rare opportunity to redefine itself. As AI agents take on more of what leadership has long involved, including decision-making, monitoring, coordination, and communication, organisations face a fundamental question: how much authority should remain with human leaders, how much should be delegated to AI, and what does this mean for leaders' legitimacy? This project studies how leaders build, lose, and rebuild legitimacy as AI takes on a growing share of leadership work. The first research stream examines how employees judge leader legitimacy when leaders rely on AI. Drawing on critical incidents of AI-supported decisions, you will investigate how different allocations of authority and oversight between leaders and AI, such as decision rights, transparency, human override, and visible oversight, shape perceptions of legitimacy. A particular focus is the boundary between AI-augmented leadership and de facto algorithmic management, and when employees perceive leadership as no longer meaningfully human-led. The second stream focuses on leaders themselves. It explores how increasing reliance on AI affects leaders' sense of identity, responsibility, and willingness to pursue leadership roles, especially when the volume of AI-mediated work exceeds what leaders can realistically evaluate. The third stream translates these insights into practice. Working with executive learners, you will design and test interventions that help leaders use AI effectively while maintaining independent judgement and accountability. The project combines critical-incident analysis, surveys, and intervention methods across organisational settings. It will generate new theory on legitimacy in AI-augmented leadership and provide practical guidance for leaders, boards, and governance bodies on responsible AI use, effective oversight, and leadership development in the age of AI. Your work Study leader-employee-AI triads and the ways leadership routines, supervisory mediation, and governance arrangements shape leader legitimacy over time. Collect and analyse critical incidents involving AI-supported decisions to understand how leaders build, lose, and regain legitimacy. Investigate when AI-augmented leadership begins to resemble de facto algorithmic management, and how employees respond to this shift. Study how increasing reliance on AI affects leader identity, responsibility, and the attractiveness of leadership roles. Design, pilot, and evaluate interventions that help leaders use AI effectively while maintaining independent judgement and accountability. Refine the research questions and design with the supervision team and prepare ethics, data-management and open-science materials. Complete a doctoral thesis and related research outputs under the host doctoral programme and EMANAIRE plan. Take part in local and network-wide training, cohort activities, reviews, dissemination and practitioner engagement. Undertake the six-month intersectoral secondment and the separate academic mobility visit. Contribute to research integrity, equality, responsible AI, open science and FAIR data practices. Who we are looking for The candidate will join BI's four-year doctoral programme, designed for talented students who wish to pursue an international academic career and contribute to the international research community through high-quality publications. Recruitment of PhD candidates is an important part of the department's research agenda and BI's ambition to be a leading European business school. Eligible applicants must have completed, or complete by the start date, a Master of Science degree with thesis work, a research master's/M.Phil. degree, or a comparable degree relevant to the PhD specialisation in Communication. Relevant disciplinary backgrounds include, but are not limited to, communication, media studies, human-computer interaction, information systems, sociology, psychology, management, organisational behaviour, human resource management, law and technology, science and technology studies, political science, or related social-science and interdisciplinary fields. An MBA degree does not fulfil the requirements for admission. For this project, the following qualities will be viewed as advantageous: Interest in leadership, organisational behaviour, legitimacy, authority, and the role of AI in organisations. Training in qualitative, quantitative, longitudinal, multilevel, or mixed-methods research. Ability to study sensitive workplace relationships and emerging technologies with methodological rigour and strong research ethics. Interest in how AI reshapes leadership, decision-making, legitimacy, and leader-employee relationships. Interest in intervention research and in developing theoretically grounded and practically relevant approaches to responsible AI-augmented leadership. Strong analytical and academic writing skills, with motivation to publish in international peer-reviewed journals. Excellent command of written and spoken English; fluency in German is a plus. Supervision and integration at BI The selected candidate will receive close supervision and follow-up from research faculty. The main supervisor is Associate Professor Petra Kipfelsberger. The candidate will be integrated into the Department of Leadership and Organizational Behavior and Department of Communication and Culture and the wider EMANAIRE network. Employment and support The four-year scholarship is currently NOK 623,200 gross per year and will be adjusted according to applicable regulations. The position normally offers membership in BI's collective pension scheme. Twenty-five per cent of working time is reserved for teaching. The candidate's workplace will be BI Norwegian Business School in Oslo, Norway. Further information about the doctoral programme and admission requirements is available from BI's PhD Administration (phd@bi.no) and the PhD specialisation in Communication: https://www.bi.no/en/programmes-and-individual-courses/phd/communication/. How to apply Candidates are asked to upload the following material with their electronic application: Certificate of a Master degree (MSc, MA, or equivalent) , including transcripts of grades. The qualifying degree must include thesis work and be completed by the start date, with a grade point average of B or higher. An MBA degree does not meet the admission requirements. Certificate of a bachelor's degree or equivalent, including transcripts of grades. BI requires a two-year master's programme and average grades of B or higher for eligibility. A Diploma Supplement , or a comparable document explaining the study and grading system and the rights for further study associated with the degree. Other relevant certificates and transcripts. A 2,000-word original project proposal that engages independently with this doctoral project, including the proposed research focus, methods, schedule and relevant literature. Any use of generative AI to produce text must be clearly stated and cited. Curriculum vitae. At least two letters of recommendation from relevant employers, professors or supervisors that provide evidence of the applicant's research potential. Certificates from previously completed doctoral-level courses, if requesting approval of these courses for the BI PhD programme at BI Norwegian Business School. If you have taken any courses at PhD level please complete the following form (maximum 18 ECTS). A complete list of publications and other documented relevant activities, where applicable. Signed Verification Document Applicants who do not hold a qualifying master's degree from a Nordic institution and whose qualifying degree was not taught in English must normally document English proficiency through TOEFL or IELTS, unless a waiver is granted. The minimum accepted scores are: TOEFL: 575 on the written test, 233 on the computer-based test, or 90 on the internet-based test. IELTS: overall score of 6.5. TOEFL and IELTS scores must be from the preceding two years. The BI institution codes are GMAT 0529, TOEFL 0529 and GRE 7365. IELTS copies are accepted when the TRF number is visible. Applicants who believe they qualify for a waiver should submit the required waiver documentation. Please note that all documents must be uploaded in the electronic application system. Documents may not be submitted by e-mail. Furthermore, documents submitted after the application deadline will not be considered. Exceptions will only be made for students who are completing their master’s degree in 2026 and do not have a final certificate when applying. Please note that applicants will not be able to revise their application once it has been submitted. Candidates may later be asked for further information and/or invited to an interview. A candidate admitted to the programme must prepare a more detailed progress plan with milestones and courses for the four years of study. MSCA eligibility Applicants may be of any nationality. To be eligible for MSCA funding, the selected candidate must, on the first day of MSCA employment: (i) not hold a doctoral degree; candidates who have successfully defended a doctoral thesis but have not yet formally received the degree are not eligible; (ii) comply with the MSCA mobility rule, meaning they must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Norway for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding that date, excluding compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent in a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention; and (iii) be enrolled in, or meet the conditions required for timely enrolment in, the doctoral programme leading to the degree specified in this notice. The appointment is conditional on documentary verification of these conditions and fulfilment of the host institution's doctoral admission requirements. More information Questions about the project can be addressed to Associate Professor Petra Kipfelsberger at petra.kipfelsberger@bi.no. Questions about the PhD specialisation in Communication can be addressed to Academic Program Manager Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no. Questions about the application process can be sent to BI's PhD Administration at phd@bi.no. Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 01/11/2026 Expected start date: 01/01/2027 Location: Oslo Contacts: Petra Kipfelsberger Mobile: +47 40291796 Company homepage: https://www.bi.no/ Handelshøyskolen BI Programområdet - Doktorgrad BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
